Purpose (task 203, finding F27): prove the narrowed, cwd-rows-only scan that
`clientCWDFieldShowsNoGhostText` uses (the version adb7db4 shipped, still in
this tree) is not merely "narrower" in a way that hides real regressions --
it, and the same bounded-scan helper (`cwdFieldRowBounds` /
`cwdFieldDimmedCell`, extracted this iteration in
features/create_cwd_ghost_test.go), still catches a ghost that should be
there. This is the positive control the negative assertion needs: a scan
that can never fail is not evidence of anything.

New step added this iteration, using the shared helpers:

    func clientCWDFieldShowsGhostText(ctx context.Context, name string) error {
        client, err := assertionClient(ctx, name)
        if err != nil {
            return err
        }
        dimmed, err := resolveScenarioTokenHex(ctx, "dimmed")
        if err != nil {
            return err
        }
        minRow, maxRow, err := cwdFieldRowBounds(client)
        if err != nil {
            return err
        }
        if _, ok := cwdFieldDimmedCell(client, dimmed); !ok {
            return fmt.Errorf("client %q has no dimmed-token cell inside the cwd field's own rows %d-%d, want a ghost there", name, minRow, maxRow)
        }
        return nil
    }

